1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.735
In this final lesson, you'll integrate

2
00:00:01.735 --> 00:00:03.770
your semantic cache into an AI agent

3
00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:06.773
so it could reuse past results, skip redundant work,

4
00:00:06.773 --> 00:00:09.776
and get faster over time. Let's have some fun.

5
00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:12.479
Before we build our own agent, let's recap.

6
00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:15.048
Agents can be expensive to scale

7
00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:17.183
because they consume a lot of tokens.

8
00:00:17.584 --> 00:00:20.487
And what makes agents unique is that during execution,

9
00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:22.589
they perform multiple steps and provide

10
00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:26.292
many opportunities to reuse intermediate states when appropriate.

11
00:00:26.593 --> 00:00:28.795
It's not just about caching the final response.

12
00:00:28.795 --> 00:00:30.897
to an original user question.

13
00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:32.999
This may work in some cases,

14
00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:36.302
but often the raw inputs to agentic applications are more complex

15
00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:39.606
and require multiple steps to get a quality answer.

16
00:00:39.606 --> 00:00:43.009
For example, agents can cache user profiles or preferences,

17
00:00:43.009 --> 00:00:45.612
the outputs to tool calls or reasoning,

18
00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:47.714
or even LLM generated plans.

19
00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:50.850
In the end, we expect to see the cache enriched over time,

20
00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:53.853
yielding fewer required tokens for processing.

21
00:00:54.354 --> 00:00:56.356
Let's see this in action. At Redis,

22
00:00:56.456 --> 00:00:58.625
we built our own Dataframe explorer agent

23
00:00:58.725 --> 00:01:01.528
that can answer questions over data sets loaded into

24
00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:03.229
pandas data frames.

25
00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.632
The agent is given a data schema

26
00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:07.934
and a user question, and these inputs prompt

27
00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:09.936
the agent to generate some Python code

28
00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:11.871
that can analyze the pandas data frame.

29
00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:14.541
Throughout the process, the agent caches multiple things,

30
00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:18.378
including end-to-end user questions and resulting answers.

31
00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:20.880
These are cached with a low TTL,

32
00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:23.983
time to live, since data can change frequently.

33
00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:27.954
We also cache the generated code that comes from the LLM.

34
00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:29.556
That way if a similar question

35
00:01:29.556 --> 00:01:31.124
is asked, we already know the code

36
00:01:31.324 --> 00:01:33.827
that can be used to execute to get the response.

37
00:01:34.227 --> 00:01:37.030
And we also cache guidance on solving common errors

38
00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:39.732
that come up from the code that's generated by the LLM.

39
00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:43.503
All of these things together can yield a more efficient agent workflow.

40
00:01:44.003 --> 00:01:47.607
So here in this diagram, we show processing three different questions

41
00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:49.476
on the left and then on

42
00:01:49.476 --> 00:01:51.211
the right. On the left, in red,

43
00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:54.013
this is the first time we've processed this question.

44
00:01:54.614 --> 00:01:56.616
And on the right, this is what happens

45
00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:59.619
when we ask a very similar question on the second pass.

46
00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:01.921
On the second pass, the cache has been enabled,

47
00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:04.124
and we can see that we use

48
00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:05.925
many less tokens the second time around.

49
00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:09.529
In the end, every single successful cycle enriches the cache

50
00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:14.300
and subsequent executions get more instant answers using less tokens overall.

51
00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:16.803
So we're going to do this ourselves in code,

52
00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:19.305
and we're going to build our own deep research agent.

53
00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.407
This agent will be built with LangGraph

54
00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:24.210
to construct the workflow to implement the cognitive architecture

55
00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.912
for agentic RAG.

56
00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:29.282
This RAG agent will also implement a quality assurance loop.

57
00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:32.585
So we'll review the answers that come from the LLM

58
00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:35.288
and using different tools, we'll revise and iterate

59
00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:37.290
and go back to the beginning if they need.

60
00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.592
Additionally, this agent will granularly cache

61
00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:43.596
individual sub-questions across executions. Let's see how this works.

62
00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:45.031
So step one with our agent

63
00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:46.699
is we're going to process the query.

64
00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:49.836
The user query comes in and we will decompose it

65
00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:52.138
into three to five sub questions.

66
00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:55.508
These you can think of as individual smaller tasks

67
00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:57.911
or smaller questions that we need to go answer.

68
00:02:58.211 --> 00:03:02.315
Secondly, we will check the semantic cache for each individual sub question.

69
00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:04.517
This allows us to see if there's

70
00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:06.519
work that's been handled in the past

71
00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:08.321
that we can reuse for this run of the agent.

72
00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:11.424
Next, we implement a research loop with tools.

73
00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:16.891
This allows us to research uncached questions using the knowledge base tool.

74
00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:20.533
Next, we evaluate the quality. The agent uses an LLM judge

75
00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:23.136
to implement a score from zero to one,

76
00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:25.038
zero being poor quality,

77
00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:27.140
one being of excellent quality.

78
00:03:27.574 --> 00:03:29.776
And if needed, we iterate and go back to the first step

79
00:03:29.776 --> 00:03:32.378
where we try to do some research again.

80
00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:35.081
We can do this up to two times with feedback.

81
00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:36.583
At the very end, the agent

82
00:03:36.583 --> 00:03:38.384
uses a large language model to combine

83
00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:41.354
all of the individual pieces of research done

84
00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:43.356
into a final response for the user.

85
00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:45.191
As you would expect, this workflow

86
00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:47.060
is expensive and takes a long time.

87
00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:50.863
to run and potentially 20, 30, 40, 50, 60 seconds,

88
00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:53.866
it just depends on the quality and difficulty of the question.

89
00:03:54.167 --> 00:03:56.269
But the result with caching included

90
00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:58.571
is that the agent is more intelligent and learns over time

91
00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:01.874
and this allows us to reduce the costs across requests

92
00:04:01.874 --> 00:04:03.676
while maintaining high response quality.

93
00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:04.978
So let's get into it and

94
00:04:04.978 --> 00:04:06.045
see how this works in the code.

95
00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:08.915
First we need to set up our environment.

96
00:04:09.282 --> 00:04:10.717
We're going to be using OpenAI

97
00:04:10.717 --> 00:04:12.385
in this lesson to build our agent.

98
00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:14.754
First we'll set our OpenAI key which

99
00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:16.389
should already be in your learning environment.

100
00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:20.393
Next, we'll go ahead and import a whole host of Python libraries

101
00:04:20.393 --> 00:04:23.196
and dependencies we'll use to build our agent in one go.

102
00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:26.966
From our Python dependencies, you'll notice we

103
00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:29.102
pull in several things from the standard library.

104
00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:31.804
We'll also pull in components from langchain,

105
00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:36.776
and LangGraph. Lastly, we'll grab necessary dependencies from Redis

106
00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:39.078
and the redis-vl open source SDK

107
00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:41.781
to do semantic caching and vectorization.

108
00:04:42.282 --> 00:04:44.484
Before we build our agent, we also need to connect

109
00:04:44.484 --> 00:04:47.787
to our Redis database running on localhost 6379.

110
00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:50.823
Let's ping to make sure we

111
00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:53.359
can talk to it. We're all good.

112
00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.462
The agent we are building is a customer support agent.

113
00:04:57.063 --> 00:04:59.132
And this agent is supposed to have access to

114
00:04:59.132 --> 00:05:01.467
a knowledge base of content in order to answer questions.

115
00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:04.737
You can think of this as content from documentation,

116
00:05:05.138 --> 00:05:06.739
from manuals, from PDFs.

117
00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:10.743
The first step is we're going to actually load our raw documentation

118
00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:13.579
into a knowledge base. This knowledge base allows

119
00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:15.982
the agent to query for the relevant information

120
00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:18.184
it needs to answer a question from scratch.

121
00:05:19.085 --> 00:05:22.188
In other words, this is allowing the agent to implement RAG.

122
00:05:22.588 --> 00:05:24.190
Let's go ahead and load our

123
00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.092
knowledge base with all of this documentation.

124
00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:29.595
Every entry in our documentation here

125
00:05:29.796 --> 00:05:33.066
is going to be vectorized using the OpenAI embedding model.

126
00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:35.902
Now that we have a knowledge base in place,

127
00:05:35.902 --> 00:05:37.370
the other thing we need in

128
00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:39.105
terms of infrastructure is the semantic cache.

129
00:05:39.706 --> 00:05:41.708
Similar to other portions in our course,

130
00:05:41.708 --> 00:05:43.943
we're going to load the semantic cache from

131
00:05:43.943 --> 00:05:46.512
our FAQ data set we've been using all along.

132
00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:52.585
Here we hydrate our semantic cache

133
00:05:52.585 --> 00:05:54.587
from the FAQ data we've been working with.

134
00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:59.292
Excellent. You can see Redis is running and accessible

135
00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:02.595
and we've loaded 8 FAQ entries into the cache.

136
00:06:04.163 --> 00:06:06.165
It's time to build our agent.

137
00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:09.135
in this lesson is built using LangGraph.

138
00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:11.070
To make this incredibly easy, we've broken

139
00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:12.939
out all of the portions of the agent

140
00:06:12.939 --> 00:06:14.640
into different helper functions.

141
00:06:14.841 --> 00:06:17.844
This method here just initializes the agent with its cache.

142
00:06:18.244 --> 00:06:20.747
knowledge base index and embedding model.

143
00:06:21.614 --> 00:06:25.952
LangGraph allows us to create workflows that pass around state.

144
00:06:26.753 --> 00:06:29.956
We're going to build this workflow using a set of helper methods

145
00:06:30.056 --> 00:06:31.758
to add nodes, to add edges,

146
00:06:32.058 --> 00:06:34.527
and direct traffic through the course of the graph.

147
00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:37.330
For example, this workflow has nodes that

148
00:06:37.597 --> 00:06:39.632
perform query decomposition,

149
00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.101
that check the cache,

150
00:06:42.802 --> 00:06:44.404
that perform additional research,

151
00:06:44.604 --> 00:06:47.407
and evaluate the quality of the research.

152
00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:50.910
And at the very end, synthesize the result for the end user.

153
00:06:51.411 --> 00:06:54.580
Our workflow entry point begins at the decompose query step,

154
00:06:54.781 --> 00:06:57.583
and we set edges between all of the necessary nodes.

155
00:06:57.784 --> 00:06:59.585
Some deterministic edges,

156
00:07:00.153 --> 00:07:02.655
and some conditional edges. Conditional edges

157
00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:05.057
are built between nodes based on some decision.

158
00:07:05.158 --> 00:07:07.660
And the decision is often specific to the workflow

159
00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:11.330
or the application at hand. Here, for example,

160
00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:14.934
We're putting a conditional edge after the cache check.

161
00:07:15.234 --> 00:07:16.636
If we need to do additional

162
00:07:16.636 --> 00:07:18.237
research on one of the decomposed questions,

163
00:07:18.538 --> 00:07:20.840
we pass the entry to the research node.

164
00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:23.843
Otherwise, if everything is already loaded from the cache,

165
00:07:23.943 --> 00:07:26.345
we can go directly to the end and skip to synthesis.

166
00:07:26.646 --> 00:07:30.349
Our agent also has a direct edge between research and evaluate quality,

167
00:07:30.349 --> 00:07:32.351
so after every single research step,

168
00:07:32.518 --> 00:07:34.720
we passed to an LLM as a judge,

169
00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.023
where we will evaluate the quality of the results.

170
00:07:37.223 --> 00:07:40.326
And lastly, after quality evaluation, we'll either go back

171
00:07:40.326 --> 00:07:42.195
to do more research or we'll go

172
00:07:42.195 --> 00:07:44.297
to the very end and synthesize the results

173
00:07:44.297 --> 00:07:47.099
for the end user. And that's where our workflow ends.

174
00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:50.603
Let's go ahead and compile this agent. Another nice property of LangGraph

175
00:07:50.703 --> 00:07:53.306
is that we can visualize the agent workflow we just built.

176
00:07:53.806 --> 00:07:55.308
As you can see, this is the

177
00:07:55.308 --> 00:07:56.809
workflow we discussed during the lesson intro.

178
00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:00.313
It involves query decomposition, cache checking, research,

179
00:08:01.013 --> 00:08:04.517
quality evaluation, and synthesis, all the way to the very end.

180
00:08:04.684 --> 00:08:06.786
It's time to demo the agent.

181
00:08:06.886 --> 00:08:09.789
We're going to test our agent against 3 different scenarios.

182
00:08:09.889 --> 00:08:11.858
The first one, an end user is

183
00:08:11.858 --> 00:08:13.793
trying to evaluate a piece of software.

184
00:08:14.193 --> 00:08:17.196
They have questions about different features and status of different things.

185
00:08:17.296 --> 00:08:19.298
They need to know before they can make the purchase.

186
00:08:19.699 --> 00:08:22.201
Let's see how the agent responds.

187
00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:29.175
Here you can see the first step

188
00:08:29.175 --> 00:08:31.143
was that the agent decomposed the original question

189
00:08:31.244 --> 00:08:33.246
into four different sub-questions.

190
00:08:33.346 --> 00:08:36.349
As you can see one, two, three, and four.

191
00:08:36.849 --> 00:08:39.652
of those four sub-questions that needed to be researched,

192
00:08:40.152 --> 00:08:41.954
three of them were not in the cache.

193
00:08:42.355 --> 00:08:43.689
But one of them, we were

194
00:08:43.689 --> 00:08:45.258
able to serve directly from the cache.

195
00:08:45.458 --> 00:08:47.126
That means we had a cache

196
00:08:47.126 --> 00:08:49.061
hit rate for this particular request, 25%.

197
00:08:49.662 --> 00:08:51.497
Now that we have three total questions that need

198
00:08:51.497 --> 00:08:53.566
to be researched because they were not in the cache,

199
00:08:54.066 --> 00:08:56.469
our agent begins its process of iterating and

200
00:08:56.469 --> 00:08:58.871
finding a result, an answer to the question.

201
00:08:59.171 --> 00:09:01.374
At the very end, once it gets to a good solution,

202
00:09:02.141 --> 00:09:04.377
all of our sub questions are validated and

203
00:09:04.377 --> 00:09:06.646
added to the cache for the next run.

204
00:09:08.047 --> 00:09:10.917
This workflow completed in about 20 seconds.

205
00:09:12.818 --> 00:09:15.721
Additionally, we used 8 large language model calls,

206
00:09:15.721 --> 00:09:19.258
two to GPT-4 and six to GPT-4-Mini.

207
00:09:19.625 --> 00:09:22.361
The total latency of the request was around 20 seconds.

208
00:09:22.929 --> 00:09:27.033
and you can see that the research portion took nearly 10 seconds.

209
00:09:27.333 --> 00:09:29.335
Here's our entire response that came back to the user.

210
00:09:29.435 --> 00:09:31.837
Personalized with all of the information that they need.

211
00:09:32.238 --> 00:09:34.040
Thank you for considering our platform.

212
00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:37.243
You can see it answer things related to the SOC 2 compliance,

213
00:09:37.743 --> 00:09:41.247
GDPR compliance, as well as API rate limits,

214
00:09:41.447 --> 00:09:43.549
and even Salesforce integration information.

215
00:09:43.816 --> 00:09:45.851
Let's move on to the next scenario.

216
00:09:46.619 --> 00:09:48.754
Scenario 2 involves a different user

217
00:09:48.754 --> 00:09:50.623
who's at a different stage of their

218
00:09:50.623 --> 00:09:52.725
buying process for this particular piece of software.

219
00:09:52.725 --> 00:09:54.727
And they need to move forward with implementation planning.

220
00:09:54.727 --> 00:09:57.363
They have some additional questions for the support agent.

221
00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.165
Let's see how this agent responds.

222
00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:03.836
In similar fashion, the agent decomposes the question

223
00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:06.238
into four sub-questions this time,

224
00:10:06.606 --> 00:10:08.741
where three are found from the cache.

225
00:10:09.542 --> 00:10:11.611
Only one of the sub-questions missed,

226
00:10:11.944 --> 00:10:13.779
and that's okay because our agent can

227
00:10:13.779 --> 00:10:15.881
handle that one question and finish its research.

228
00:10:17.416 --> 00:10:18.985
At the very end, you can see

229
00:10:18.985 --> 00:10:20.786
we get a cache hit rate of 75%.

230
00:10:21.087 --> 00:10:23.189
Three of the four questions hit the cache.

231
00:10:23.689 --> 00:10:25.758
In turn, that took our LLM calls from

232
00:10:25.758 --> 00:10:28.094
eight in the previous run down to just four.

233
00:10:28.828 --> 00:10:32.732
two to GPT-4, two to GPT-4-Mini.

234
00:10:33.232 --> 00:10:35.434
The total latency was also 13 seconds on this run

235
00:10:35.434 --> 00:10:37.737
instead of around 20 to 25 on

236
00:10:37.737 --> 00:10:40.039
the last one. That's a big improvement.

237
00:10:40.272 --> 00:10:41.807
And here you can see the

238
00:10:41.807 --> 00:10:43.576
nicely formatted response to the end user.

239
00:10:44.076 --> 00:10:47.279
This calls out things like rate limits, Salesforce integration capabilities,

240
00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:49.782
data export options and payment methods.

241
00:10:50.082 --> 00:10:52.084
These are all things that this user requested

242
00:10:52.084 --> 00:10:54.086
in order to plan out their software implementation.

243
00:10:54.887 --> 00:10:58.491
Let's go to the final scenario. In our third and final scenario,

244
00:10:58.591 --> 00:11:02.294
this is yet another user that is doing a pre-purchase comprehensive review.

245
00:11:02.695 --> 00:11:04.597
They're at the final stage of procurement and

246
00:11:04.597 --> 00:11:06.499
before they can do the Pro plan purchase,

247
00:11:06.599 --> 00:11:09.902
they want to complete validation on different things they have questions about.

248
00:11:10.202 --> 00:11:12.104
You'll notice some of these things are similar

249
00:11:12.104 --> 00:11:13.973
topics that came up in other user's requests.

250
00:11:14.273 --> 00:11:16.676
Let's see how our agent handles this particular request.

251
00:11:19.078 --> 00:11:21.380
Similar to before, our agent handles this

252
00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:23.983
question by breaking it into four sub questions.

253
00:11:24.383 --> 00:11:26.385
Similar to last time, three of our four questions

254
00:11:26.385 --> 00:11:28.387
are hit on the cache and one is missed.

255
00:11:28.587 --> 00:11:30.523
Again, this is not a problem because our

256
00:11:30.523 --> 00:11:32.692
agent can still handle research on that one task.

257
00:11:35.661 --> 00:11:38.497
And at the very end, you can see our total latency

258
00:11:39.065 --> 00:11:40.566
is around 18 seconds.

259
00:11:40.766 --> 00:11:42.268
LLM calls six this time,

260
00:11:42.568 --> 00:11:44.870
not as good as four, but still better than eight.

261
00:11:45.071 --> 00:11:48.874
The cache hit rate for this particular request was around 75%.

262
00:11:49.475 --> 00:11:52.578
Now that we've run our agent across different scenarios, you're probably wondering,

263
00:11:52.678 --> 00:11:55.181
how do they compare head-to-head? Well, let's take a look.

264
00:11:55.381 --> 00:11:57.583
Here, this function analyze_agent_results

265
00:11:57.983 --> 00:12:01.053
takes the results of all three scenarios that we tested

266
00:12:01.053 --> 00:12:04.156
and reports back a nice plot and visualization.

267
00:12:04.156 --> 00:12:05.891
Let's take a look. On scenario one,

268
00:12:05.891 --> 00:12:07.860
we had a cache hit rate of 25%.

269
00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:09.528
But in the next two scenarios we

270
00:12:09.528 --> 00:12:11.163
hit our cache 75% of the time.

271
00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:13.599
You can see the cumulative cache hit

272
00:12:13.599 --> 00:12:15.634
rate over time gets up to 60%.

273
00:12:15.735 --> 00:12:18.037
We expect over time in a system with

274
00:12:18.037 --> 00:12:20.339
hundreds or thousands or even millions of users,

275
00:12:20.439 --> 00:12:23.142
this particular cache hit rate can be very effective

276
00:12:23.142 --> 00:12:25.845
at saving costs. Let's look at the LLM calls.

277
00:12:26.045 --> 00:12:28.180
In our first scenario, we used up 8

278
00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:30.349
different calls to different LLMs for different tasks.

279
00:12:30.783 --> 00:12:33.185
But in the last two, we used four

280
00:12:33.185 --> 00:12:35.588
and six respectively. This is a great savings.

281
00:12:36.288 --> 00:12:38.257
And probably the most interesting piece

282
00:12:38.257 --> 00:12:40.559
for our user experience is the latency.

283
00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:43.162
you can see in the first scenario

284
00:12:43.162 --> 00:12:45.164
our overall latency was quite large.

285
00:12:45.464 --> 00:12:47.266
and in the next two scenarios,

286
00:12:47.266 --> 00:12:48.601
the latency end to end was

287
00:12:48.601 --> 00:12:50.169
about a third of the total latency.

288
00:12:50.369 --> 00:12:52.371
This is a significant performance improvement

289
00:12:52.571 --> 00:12:55.374
and again over time we expect this kind of trend to continue.

290
00:12:55.808 --> 00:12:57.710
Now we're going to build an interactive demo

291
00:12:57.710 --> 00:12:59.612
that's going to bring all of this together.

292
00:13:00.112 --> 00:13:03.215
The demo runs on Gradio and allows us to reach out

293
00:13:03.315 --> 00:13:06.318
to external web pages at some URL of your choice.

294
00:13:06.619 --> 00:13:09.622
We'll pull in all of the documentation and content from those pages,

295
00:13:09.822 --> 00:13:11.924
pull it into a knowledge base for your agent.

296
00:13:12.224 --> 00:13:14.827
And the agent is going to implement agentic RAG

297
00:13:14.827 --> 00:13:16.829
and allow you to chat with those documents.

298
00:13:17.029 --> 00:13:20.733
And over time, this particular agent will build up its semantic cache

299
00:13:20.733 --> 00:13:22.234
just like we've seen in theory

300
00:13:22.234 --> 00:13:24.036
and practice in the lessons leading up.

301
00:13:30.309 --> 00:13:33.646
Now we have our deep research agent enabled with semantic caching.

302
00:13:34.213 --> 00:13:35.881
Let's pull in the contents from

303
00:13:35.881 --> 00:13:37.817
the AT&amp;T International cell phone plan site.

304
00:13:41.887 --> 00:13:43.989
Hitting process URL will first crawl and

305
00:13:43.989 --> 00:13:46.392
load all of that content from the website

306
00:13:46.692 --> 00:13:48.994
and load it into our local knowledge base.

307
00:13:49.395 --> 00:13:52.698
Now we can ask different questions of this particular data,

308
00:13:52.898 --> 00:13:54.934
implementing the exact same agent that

309
00:13:54.934 --> 00:13:57.269
we saw before. Let's try one question.

310
00:13:57.269 --> 00:13:59.038
The first question we have for our

311
00:13:59.038 --> 00:14:01.073
agent is about a cruise trip coming up.

312
00:14:01.173 --> 00:14:02.541
We want to make sure that our

313
00:14:02.541 --> 00:14:04.076
cell phone coverage through AT&amp;T will still work

314
00:14:04.176 --> 00:14:06.378
while we're on the cruise. Will we be okay?

315
00:14:09.315 --> 00:14:11.317
The agent is currently doing its research

316
00:14:11.317 --> 00:14:13.319
to find an answer to this question.

317
00:14:13.919 --> 00:14:17.223
We can see in the performance log, nothing registered on the cache,

318
00:14:17.423 --> 00:14:18.824
and it overall took about four

319
00:14:18.824 --> 00:14:20.426
LLM calls to get to an answer.

320
00:14:21.727 --> 00:14:23.729
Let's try another question.

321
00:14:23.762 --> 00:14:26.232
When traveling internationally, is there any difference between cruise

322
00:14:26.232 --> 00:14:28.734
and land in terms of AT&amp;T coverage?

323
00:14:29.735 --> 00:14:31.737
All right.

324
00:14:31.737 --> 00:14:33.739
On this one, we were actually able to leverage the cache.

325
00:14:33.939 --> 00:14:36.442
One sub question from the LLM, hit on the cache.

326
00:14:36.742 --> 00:14:39.044
We only used two LLM calls end to end.

327
00:14:39.445 --> 00:14:40.946
And you can see here, we've got

328
00:14:40.946 --> 00:14:42.448
a nice personalized answer to the user.

329
00:14:42.748 --> 00:14:44.350
Let's try another question.

330
00:14:44.383 --> 00:14:47.653
in Spain, will I be covered by AT&amp;T still?

331
00:14:49.154 --> 00:14:51.156
Nice.

332
00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:52.625
The agent tells us yes, we can

333
00:14:52.625 --> 00:14:54.260
still use our AT&amp;T service while in Spain.

334
00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:57.897
AT&amp;T offers an International Day Pass. Excellent.

335
00:14:57.897 --> 00:14:59.531
And here on this particular question, we

336
00:14:59.531 --> 00:15:01.200
also used an entry in the cache.

337
00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:04.003
And only two LLM calls. We're around 300 tokens.

338
00:15:04.703 --> 00:15:06.906
That's great. Let's now ask a more

339
00:15:06.906 --> 00:15:09.074
complicated question about our trip to Spain.

340
00:15:09.975 --> 00:15:12.044
We've got a trip coming up in Spain where part of the

341
00:15:12.044 --> 00:15:14.079
trip is on a cruise and the other part is on land.

342
00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:17.683
What options are there for me to keep cell coverage through AT&amp;T?

343
00:15:19.018 --> 00:15:21.186
Here we can see the agent responded with

344
00:15:21.186 --> 00:15:23.656
a personalized answer about my upcoming trip to Spain.

345
00:15:24.556 --> 00:15:26.091
It notices that I'm going to be

346
00:15:26.091 --> 00:15:27.860
spending time on land and on a cruise.

347
00:15:28.527 --> 00:15:30.095
On this particular question we also were

348
00:15:30.095 --> 00:15:31.630
able to utilize something in the cache.

349
00:15:31.931 --> 00:15:33.065
And so the LLM was more

350
00:15:33.065 --> 00:15:34.333
efficient than the first time we ran.

351
00:15:39.438 --> 00:15:41.440
Let's take a look at what ended up in our cache.

352
00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:43.342
We can do a cursory cache

353
00:15:43.342 --> 00:15:45.244
check with a distance threshold of one,

354
00:15:45.244 --> 00:15:49.248
meaning find everything, you know, anywhere near this particular query.

355
00:15:50.549 --> 00:15:53.252
Here we can see the entry in the cache was

356
00:15:53.352 --> 00:15:55.554
I have a cruise ship coming up and wanted to make sure

357
00:15:55.654 --> 00:15:57.356
that my cell phone coverage through AT&amp;T will work.

358
00:15:57.656 --> 00:15:59.525
We have a response that was used

359
00:15:59.825 --> 00:16:03.329
looks like multiple times throughout the course of this particular agent execution.

360
00:16:03.796 --> 00:16:06.465
Now that you have access to this agent in the demo,

361
00:16:06.765 --> 00:16:08.767
try another URL, try another site,

362
00:16:08.968 --> 00:16:12.571
ask different questions, experiment with how this agent performs over time.

363
00:16:12.771 --> 00:16:15.474
You have what you need here to build a production ready

364
00:16:15.574 --> 00:16:18.177
AI agent that uses a semantic cache.

365
00:16:18.377 --> 00:16:19.778
Along the way, we hope you learned

366
00:16:19.778 --> 00:16:21.180
about a lot of the great fundamentals

367
00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:23.282
in order to build and scale the system.

